# Delta Lake Storage Costs: Time Travel, VACUUM, and the Real Bill

Time travel is one of Delta Lake's best features — until your cloud
storage bill arrives. Every UPDATE, DELETE, and MERGE keeps old data
files around so you can query historical versions. That's powerful
for debugging, auditing, and recovery. It's also storage you're
paying for.

This model works through the real numbers for a 500-turbine wind
utility running a medallion pipeline on Delta Lake, then shows
exactly when VACUUM frequency becomes a financial decision.

## The bottom line

| | 7-day VACUUM | 30-day VACUUM | 90-day VACUUM |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Year 1 storage cost** | **{{yr1_cost_7d}}** | **{{yr1_cost_30d}}** | **{{yr1_cost_90d}}** |
| **Year 3 storage cost** | **{{yr3_cost_7d}}** | **{{yr3_cost_30d}}** | **{{yr3_cost_90d}}** |
| **Storage multiplier vs. current-only** | {{multiplier_7d}}x | {{multiplier_30d}}x | {{multiplier_90d}}x |
| **Monthly at Year 1** | {{monthly_7d}} | {{monthly_30d}} | {{monthly_90d}} |

The base data (current version only) costs about {{current_only_annual}}/year
in S3 storage. Time travel overhead with 30-day retention roughly
doubles that — not catastrophic, but not free. At 90 days (common
for NERC audit compliance), you're paying about {{multiplier_90d}}x.

**Storage crosses $1K/month** at roughly {{break_even_ingest}} of
daily ingest with 30-day retention.

**The rest of this document shows how we got here.** Change any
assumption and rerun.

---

## Part 1: Base Storage (Current Version Only)

### Daily ingest

We carry forward the Module 1 sizing: 500 turbines, ~100 SCADA
signals each, 10-minute polling intervals. After columnar compression
at 8x, that's about 2 GB/day landing as Parquet files inside Delta
tables. (See `wind-utility-sizing.cm` for the full derivation.)

daily_ingest = 2 GB

### Medallion layer sizes

**Bronze** is append-only — every reading lands here, never modified.
It grows linearly at the daily ingest rate.

**Silver** applies quality checks: null readings, out-of-range values,
duplicate timestamps. About 20% of raw SCADA readings get rejected
(source: operational experience — SCADA systems routinely produce
5–25% bad readings from sensor drift, communication dropouts, and
maintenance windows). Silver retains 80% of Bronze volume.

**Gold** aggregates Silver into business-ready tables: hourly capacity
factors, daily availability metrics, monthly fleet summaries. The
aggregation reduces volume to about 30% of Silver.

bronze_daily = daily_ingest
silver_daily = 80% of bronze_daily
gold_daily = 30% of silver_daily

current_daily = bronze_daily + silver_daily + gold_daily

Over a year, the current-version-only storage accumulates:

current_annual_gb = current_daily/day over 1 year

S3 Standard pricing in us-east-1 is $0.023/GB/month
([AWS S3 pricing](https://aws.amazon.com/s3/pricing/)):

s3_rate = $0.023

current_only_monthly = s3_rate * number(current_annual_gb) / 1073741824
current_only_annual = current_only_monthly * 12

---

## Part 2: Time Travel Overhead

Here's where Delta Lake's versioning costs real money. Every write
operation that modifies existing data creates NEW Parquet files
while keeping the OLD ones around for time travel.

### Bronze: almost no overhead

Bronze is append-only. New data lands as new files — nothing gets
rewritten. Old versions of Bronze ARE the current data minus later
appends. The transaction log grows (one small JSON file per commit),
but the data files themselves aren't duplicated.

bronze_overhead_daily = 0 GB

### Silver: MERGE creates copies

Silver runs a daily MERGE: match incoming Bronze records against
existing Silver records by turbine_id + timestamp, update quality
flags, insert new rows. Each MERGE rewrites the affected data files.
For a daily reprocessing window that touches most of the table,
this creates roughly 80% of Silver's current size in new files per
day (the old files stick around for time travel).

silver_overhead_daily = 80% of silver_daily

### Gold: full overwrite each day

Gold tables are typically overwritten daily — recompute yesterday's
aggregates from Silver. An overwrite (INSERT OVERWRITE or
TRUNCATE + INSERT) marks all old files as removed and writes a
complete new set. That's 100% overhead per day.

gold_overhead_daily = gold_daily

### Total daily overhead

overhead_daily = bronze_overhead_daily + silver_overhead_daily + gold_overhead_daily

This overhead accumulates for as many days as your VACUUM retention
allows. VACUUM deletes files no longer referenced by any version
within the retention window.

---

## Part 3: VACUUM Retention Trade-offs

### 7-day retention

Aggressive. Saves storage but limits recovery. If a bad pipeline
deploy corrupts Silver on Monday and nobody notices until the
following Monday, you can't roll back.

retention_7d = 7
overhead_7d = overhead_daily * retention_7d
total_stored_7d = current_annual_gb + overhead_7d
multiplier_7d = number(total_stored_7d) / number(current_annual_gb)

### 30-day retention (Delta Lake default)

The sweet spot for most teams. Covers month-end close, most incident
discovery windows, and gives comfortable margin for debugging
pipeline issues.

retention_30d = 30
overhead_30d = overhead_daily * retention_30d
total_stored_30d = current_annual_gb + overhead_30d
multiplier_30d = number(total_stored_30d) / number(current_annual_gb)

### 90-day retention

Expensive, but NERC CIP audits may require proving data lineage
over a quarter. If your compliance team needs to answer "show me
exactly what this table looked like on March 15th," 90-day retention
is the easy path.

retention_90d = 90
overhead_90d = overhead_daily * retention_90d
total_stored_90d = current_annual_gb + overhead_90d
multiplier_90d = number(total_stored_90d) / number(current_annual_gb)

### Annual costs by retention

yr1_cost_7d = s3_rate * number(total_stored_7d) / 1073741824 * 12
yr1_cost_30d = s3_rate * number(total_stored_30d) / 1073741824 * 12
yr1_cost_90d = s3_rate * number(total_stored_90d) / 1073741824 * 12

monthly_7d = yr1_cost_7d / 12
monthly_30d = yr1_cost_30d / 12
monthly_90d = yr1_cost_90d / 12

---

## Part 4: 3-Year Projection

US wind capacity has grown ~10% annually over the past decade
(source: ACP/AWEA annual market reports). As the fleet grows,
so does every layer of storage:

yr1_base = total_stored_30d
yr3_base = compound yr1_base by 10% over 3 years

yr3_cost_7d = compound yr1_cost_7d by 10% over 3 years
yr3_cost_30d = compound yr1_cost_30d by 10% over 3 years
yr3_cost_90d = compound yr1_cost_90d by 10% over 3 years

yr3_monthly_30d = yr3_cost_30d / 12

---

## Part 5: When to Worry

### When does storage exceed $1K/month?

At $0.023/GB/month with a 30-day VACUUM multiplier of
{{multiplier_30d}}x, the break-even daily ingest for a $1K/month
storage bill:

target_monthly = $1000
effective_multiplier = multiplier_30d
break_even_gb = number(target_monthly) / number(s3_rate) / number(effective_multiplier)
break_even_ingest = break_even_gb / 365 * 1 GB

Our wind utility at {{daily_ingest}}/day is well under that. Storage
is NOT the expensive line item — compute (SQL warehouses, ETL
clusters) dominates the Databricks bill by 10–20x. But storage
costs are sneaky because they only go up: compute scales to zero
when idle, storage doesn't.

### When does VACUUM frequency matter financially?

The difference between 7-day and 90-day retention:

retention_spread_annual = yr1_cost_90d - yr1_cost_7d

That's {{retention_spread_annual}}/year — the price of audit-friendly
retention. For a wind utility under NERC CIP, that's pocket change
compared to the $1M+ fine for not being able to demonstrate data
lineage.

### The real VACUUM decision

VACUUM isn't primarily a cost decision. It's a **recovery window**
decision. The questions to ask:

- How fast does your team notice pipeline bugs? (7 days? 30 days?)
- Does your compliance framework require historical point-in-time queries?
- Do downstream consumers (ML models, reports) need reproducible results from last month?

Set retention to match your longest incident discovery window, not
your storage budget. If storage becomes a real problem, the fix is
usually partitioning + targeted VACUUM on specific tables — not
a blanket reduction in retention.

---

## Detailed Breakdown (computed)

| Layer | Daily current | Daily overhead | 30-day overhead total |
|-------|--------------|----------------|----------------------|
| Bronze (append-only) | {{bronze_daily}} | {{bronze_overhead_daily}} | — |
| Silver (daily MERGE) | {{silver_daily}} | {{silver_overhead_daily}} | {{silver_overhead_30d}} |
| Gold (daily overwrite) | {{gold_daily}} | {{gold_overhead_daily}} | {{gold_overhead_30d}} |
| **Total** | **{{current_daily}}** | **{{overhead_daily}}** | **{{overhead_30d}}** |

silver_overhead_30d = silver_overhead_daily * retention_30d
gold_overhead_30d = gold_overhead_daily * retention_30d

### Try it yourself

Change `daily_ingest = 20 GB` at the top (a larger fleet, or
higher-frequency SCADA polling) and rerun. Or set all three
retentions to 1 day to see the theoretical minimum storage cost.
The point isn't the exact numbers — it's understanding which knob
moves which cost.
